  "repository": {
    "frontmatter": {
      "begin": "\\A---\\s*$",
      "end": "^---\\s*$",
      "name": "meta.frontmatter.carve",
      "beginCaptures": {
        "0": {
          "name": "punctuation.definition.frontmatter.carve"
        }
      },
      "endCaptures": {
        "0": {
          "name": "punctuation.definition.frontmatter.carve"
        }
      },
      "patterns": [
        {
          "match": "^([A-Za-z_][\\w-]*)\\s*(:)",
          "captures": {
            "1": {
              "name": "entity.name.tag.yaml.carve"
            },
            "2": {
              "name": "punctuation.separator.key-value.yaml.carve"
            }
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    "fenced_code": {
      "begin": "^(\\s*)(`{3,}|~{3,})\\s*(=?[a-zA-Z0-9_-]*)\\s*(\"[^\"\\n]*\")?\\s*(\\[[^\\]\\n]*\\])?\\s*$",
      "beginCaptures": {
        "2": {
          "name": "punctuation.definition.fenced.carve"
        },
        "3": {
          "name": "fenced_code.block.language.carve"
        },
        "4": {
          "name": "string.quoted.double.fenced.title.carve"
        },
        "5": {
          "name": "constant.other.label.fenced.carve"
        }
      },
      "end": "^\\1\\2\\s*$",
      "endCaptures": {
        "0": {
          "name": "punctuation.definition.fenced.carve"
        }
      },
      "name": "markup.fenced_code.block.carve",
      "contentName": "markup.raw.code.carve",
      "patterns": [
        {
          "include": "#callout_marker"
        }
      ],
      "comment": "The leading-whitespace capture is load-bearing twice over: the `end` pattern closes on the SAME indentation via \\1, and a fence inside a list item is indented. Leading whitespace is ALLOWED, deliberately (carve-grammars#71). The strict column-0 rule makes an indented opener literal text at the top level, but the same opener indented INSIDE a list item is valid and common - verified against carve-rs: at the top level every one of these renders as a paragraph, and at a list item's content column every one opens its real block. A line-based grammar cannot tell those two apart; that needs block context, which only tree-sitter-carve has (its #38). So the choice is over-coloring the rare invalid case or under-coloring the common valid one, and every block rule here picks the same side - the asymmetry this used to have was an accident of anchoring, not a decision."
    },
    "heading": {
      "match": "^[ \\t]*(#{1,6}) +(?![ \\t]*$)(.*?)[ \\t]*$",
      "captures": {
        "1": {
          "name": "punctuation.definition.heading.carve"
        },
        "2": {
          "name": "entity.name.section.carve"
        }
      },
      "name": "markup.heading.carve",
      "comment": "Leading whitespace is ALLOWED, deliberately (carve-grammars#71). The strict column-0 rule makes an indented opener literal text at the top level, but the same opener indented INSIDE a list item is valid and common - verified against carve-rs: at the top level every one of these renders as a paragraph, and at a list item's content column every one opens its real block. A line-based grammar cannot tell those two apart; that needs block context, which only tree-sitter-carve has (its #38). So the choice is over-coloring the rare invalid case or under-coloring the common valid one, and every block rule here picks the same side - the asymmetry this used to have was an accident of anchoring, not a decision."
    },
    "hr": {
      "match": "^\\s*(?:-{3,}|\\*{3,}|_{3,})\\s*$",
      "name": "meta.separator.thematic-break.carve"
    },
    "block_comment": {
      "comment": "A %%% fence line is a delimiter plus an insignificant tail (spec PART 9 section 28): only the leading run of % is structural, so `%%% TODO` opens and `%%% end` closes. %%% carries no info string - a raw passthrough block is a CODE fence with an =FORMAT info string - so `%%% html` is a comment. The end backreference matches the opener width exactly, so %%%% nests %%%.",
      "begin": "^\\s*(%{3,})[^\\n]*$",
      "end": "^\\s*\\1(?!%)[^\\n]*$",
      "name": "comment.block.carve"
    },
    "line_comment": {
      "match": "^\\s*%%(?!%).*$",
      "name": "comment.line.percent.carve"
    },
    "trailing_comment": {
      "match": "(?<=[ \\t])%%.*$",
      "name": "comment.line.percent.carve"
    },
    "admonition": {
      "match": "^[ \\t]*(:{3,})(?:[ \\t]*(\\||\\\\)|[ \\t]*([a-zA-Z_][\\w-]*)(?:[ \\t]+(\"[^\"\\n]*\"))?(?:[ \\t]+(\\[[^\\]\\n]*\\]))?|[ \\t]*(\\[[^\\]\\n]*\\]))?[ \\t]*$",
      "captures": {
        "1": {
          "name": "punctuation.definition.admonition.carve"
        },
        "2": {
          "name": "entity.name.tag.admonition.carve"
        },
        "3": {
          "name": "entity.name.tag.admonition.carve"
        },
        "4": {
          "name": "string.quoted.double.admonition.title.carve"
        },
        "5": {
          "name": "constant.other.label.admonition.carve"
        },
        "6": {
          "name": "constant.other.label.admonition.carve"
        }
      },
      "name": "meta.admonition.carve",
      "comment": "Leading whitespace is ALLOWED, deliberately (carve-grammars#71). The strict column-0 rule makes an indented opener literal text at the top level, but the same opener indented INSIDE a list item is valid and common - verified against carve-rs: at the top level every one of these renders as a paragraph, and at a list item's content column every one opens its real block. A line-based grammar cannot tell those two apart; that needs block context, which only tree-sitter-carve has (its #38). So the choice is over-coloring the rare invalid case or under-coloring the common valid one, and every block rule here picks the same side - the asymmetry this used to have was an accident of anchoring, not a decision."
    },
    "blockquote": {
      "match": "^[ \\t]*(>)(?= |$) ?(.*)$",
      "captures": {
        "1": {
          "name": "punctuation.definition.quote.carve"
        },
        "2": {
          "name": "markup.quote.carve"
        }
      },
      "name": "markup.quote.carve",
      "comment": "Leading whitespace is ALLOWED, deliberately (carve-grammars#71). The strict column-0 rule makes an indented opener literal text at the top level, but the same opener indented INSIDE a list item is valid and common - verified against carve-rs: at the top level every one of these renders as a paragraph, and at a list item's content column every one opens its real block. A line-based grammar cannot tell those two apart; that needs block context, which only tree-sitter-carve has (its #38). So the choice is over-coloring the rare invalid case or under-coloring the common valid one, and every block rule here picks the same side - the asymmetry this used to have was an accident of anchoring, not a decision."
    },
    "caption": {
      "match": "^[ \\t]*(\\^) +(?![ \\t]*$)(.*)$",
      "captures": {
        "1": {
          "name": "punctuation.definition.caption.carve"
        },
        "2": {
          "name": "markup.caption.carve"
        }
      },
      "name": "meta.caption.carve",
      "comment": "Leading whitespace is ALLOWED, deliberately (carve-grammars#71). The strict column-0 rule makes an indented opener literal text at the top level, but the same opener indented INSIDE a list item is valid and common - verified against carve-rs: at the top level every one of these renders as a paragraph, and at a list item's content column every one opens its real block. A line-based grammar cannot tell those two apart; that needs block context, which only tree-sitter-carve has (its #38). So the choice is over-coloring the rare invalid case or under-coloring the common valid one, and every block rule here picks the same side - the asymmetry this used to have was an accident of anchoring, not a decision."
    },
    "abbreviation": {
      "match": "^[ \\t]*(\\*)(\\[)([A-Z][A-Z0-9]*)(\\])(:) +(.*)$",
      "captures": {
        "1": {
          "name": "punctuation.definition.abbreviation.carve"
        },
        "2": {
          "name": "punctuation.definition.abbreviation.carve"
        },
        "3": {
          "name": "entity.name.abbreviation.carve"
        },
        "4": {
          "name": "punctuation.definition.abbreviation.carve"
        },
        "5": {
          "name": "punctuation.separator.carve"
        },
        "6": {
          "name": "string.unquoted.abbreviation.carve"
        }
      },
      "name": "meta.abbreviation.carve",
      "comment": "Leading whitespace is ALLOWED, deliberately (carve-grammars#71). The strict column-0 rule makes an indented opener literal text at the top level, but the same opener indented INSIDE a list item is valid and common - verified against carve-rs: at the top level every one of these renders as a paragraph, and at a list item's content column every one opens its real block. A line-based grammar cannot tell those two apart; that needs block context, which only tree-sitter-carve has (its #38). So the choice is over-coloring the rare invalid case or under-coloring the common valid one, and every block rule here picks the same side - the asymmetry this used to have was an accident of anchoring, not a decision."
    },
    "link_ref_def": {
      "match": "^([ \\t]*)(\\[)(?![@^])([^\\]]+)(\\])(:) +(\\S+)(?:[ \\t]+(\"(?:[^\"\\\\]|\\\\.)*\"|'(?:[^'\\\\]|\\\\.)*'))?",
      "captures": {
        "2": {
          "name": "punctuation.definition.link.carve"
        },
        "3": {
          "name": "constant.other.reference.link.carve"
        },
        "4": {
          "name": "punctuation.definition.link.carve"
        },
        "5": {
          "name": "punctuation.separator.carve"
        },
        "6": {
          "name": "markup.underline.link.carve"
        },
        "7": {
          "name": "string.quoted.link.title.carve"
        }
      },
      "name": "meta.link.reference.def.carve"
    },
    "table_row": {
      "begin": "^[ \\t]*(?=\\|)",
      "end": "$",
      "patterns": [
        {
          "match": "(\\|=)([<~>]?)",
          "captures": {
            "1": {
              "name": "keyword.operator.table.header.carve"
            },
            "2": {
              "name": "keyword.operator.table.alignment.carve"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "match": "(\\|)([<~>])(?=\\s)",
          "captures": {
            "1": {
              "name": "punctuation.separator.table.carve"
            },
            "2": {
              "name": "keyword.operator.table.alignment.carve"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "match": "(?<=\\|)\\s*(\\^)\\s*(?=\\|)",
          "captures": {
            "1": {
              "name": "keyword.operator.table.rowspan.carve"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "match": "(?<=\\|)\\s*(<)\\s*(?=\\|)",
          "captures": {
            "1": {
              "name": "keyword.operator.table.colspan.carve"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "comment": "Column alignment markers (:--, --:, :-:, ---) between pipes. Matched before #inline so smart-typography does not swallow the dashes.",
          "match": "(?<=\\|)\\s*(:?-+:?)\\s*(?=\\|)",
          "captures": {
            "1": {
              "name": "keyword.operator.table.alignment.carve"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "match": "\\|",
          "name": "punctuation.separator.table.carve"
        },
        {
          "include": "#inline"
        }
      ],
      "comment": "Leading whitespace is ALLOWED, deliberately (carve-grammars#71). The strict column-0 rule makes an indented opener literal text at the top level, but the same opener indented INSIDE a list item is valid and common - verified against carve-rs: at the top level every one of these renders as a paragraph, and at a list item's content column every one opens its real block. A line-based grammar cannot tell those two apart; that needs block context, which only tree-sitter-carve has (its #38). So the choice is over-coloring the rare invalid case or under-coloring the common valid one, and every block rule here picks the same side - the asymmetry this used to have was an accident of anchoring, not a decision."
    },
    "multiline_cell": {
      "match": "^[ \\t]*(\\+)(.*)$",
      "captures": {
        "1": {
          "name": "keyword.operator.table.continuation.carve"
        }
      },
      "comment": "Leading whitespace is ALLOWED, deliberately (carve-grammars#71). The strict column-0 rule makes an indented opener literal text at the top level, but the same opener indented INSIDE a list item is valid and common - verified against carve-rs: at the top level every one of these renders as a paragraph, and at a list item's content column every one opens its real block. A line-based grammar cannot tell those two apart; that needs block context, which only tree-sitter-carve has (its #38). So the choice is over-coloring the rare invalid case or under-coloring the common valid one, and every block rule here picks the same side - the asymmetry this used to have was an accident of anchoring, not a decision."
    },
    "task_item": {
      "match": "^([ \\t]*)([-*]) +(\\[)([ xX\\-_>?])(\\]) +(?![ \\t]*$)",
      "captures": {
        "2": {
          "name": "punctuation.definition.list.carve"
        },
        "3": {
          "name": "punctuation.definition.checkbox.carve"
        },
        "4": {
          "name": "constant.language.checkbox.carve"
        },
        "5": {
          "name": "punctuation.definition.checkbox.carve"
        }
      }
    },
    "list_continuation": {
      "comment": "A lone `+` at the list-marker column is the list-continuation marker (not a bullet -- `+` is never a bullet in Carve); it attaches the following flush-left block to the item.",
      "match": "^(\\s*)(\\+)(\\s*)$",
      "captures": {
        "2": {
          "name": "punctuation.definition.list.continuation.carve"
        }
      }
    },
    "list_item": {
      "match": "^([ \\t]*)([-*])( +)(?![ \\t]*$)((?:[-*] +)*)",
      "captures": {
        "2": {
          "name": "punctuation.definition.list.unnumbered.carve"
        },
        "4": {
          "name": "punctuation.definition.list.unnumbered.carve"
        }
      },
      "comment": "A marker line may carry several markers (`- - A` opens two nested lists), so scope each one, not only the line-anchored first."
    },
    "numbered_item": {
      "comment": "Every ordered marker the language accepts, not only `1.`: decimal, single-letter and roman, with `.` or `)`, plus the BARE DOT (carve#472) - `.` alone continues the ordered sequence, and is the only marker allowed to drop its value. The bare form is also recognised glued to an attribute block (`.{#x}`), which is how corpus 174-bare-dot-ordered-markers-3 writes it.",
      "match": "^([ \\t]*)((?:\\d+|[A-Za-z]|[ivxlcdmIVXLCDM]+)[.)]|\\.)(?= |\\{)( *)(?![ \\t]*$)",
      "captures": {
        "2": {
          "name": "punctuation.definition.list.numbered.carve"
        }
      }
    },
